<section class="ls-manifesto-chapter" id="principles">
<header class="ls-chapter-heading ls-container">
<p class="ls-eyebrow">02 / The principles</p>
<h2>MORE CONTINUITY. <span class="ls-stroke">LESS UPKEEP.</span>
</h2>
</header>
<div class="ls-story-unit" data-tone="yellow" data-watermark="mark">
<div class="ls-container">
<div class="ls-principle-pair">
<article class="ls-principle" data-principle="true" id="own-file">
<span class="ls-principle-index">04 /</span>
<h3>YOUR FILE COMES FIRST.</h3>
<p>The document is a Markdown file you own. Read it, edit it, keep it. Your work should remain useful when you change a model, pause a subscription or choose another tool. Make the file the foundation.</p>
</article>
<article class="ls-principle" data-principle="true" id="visible-boundary">
<span class="ls-principle-index">05 /</span>
<h3>NAME THE BOUNDARY.</h3>
<p>Choose the files, folders and public pages the job may use. State which parts may change. A model, a network request and a script have different permissions. Delegation becomes practical when the assignment is specific.</p>
</article>
</div>
